Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
[snip discussion of Renee having no $TERM variable]
> It's an environment variable, but I imagine it 
> would need to be set on the server, in order for
> programs running on the server to see it.  I
> don't think the environment from your local
> system would be inherited by processes to which
> you connect via the network (ssh, telnet, etc).
This is normally the case, but $TERM is special. ssh will try to send
$TERM from the local machine to the server directly, and telnet also
sends it (but in a roundabout way).

[snip]
> How you set an environment variable depends on
> your shell.  I happen to be using bash, so I could 
> set an environment variable temporarily with the export
> command or more permanently in my .bashrc file.  YMMV.
[snip]

Agreed. If Renee's terminal is bash (it looks like it based on their
prompt), they can set $TERM to an appropriate value like this:

For ssh to Linux-based systems (e.g. nethack.alt.org):

export TERM=xterm

For ssh to BSD-based systems (e.g. /dev/null/nethack's servers):

export TERM=xtermcolor

The export command should be run before the ssh command.

